{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Increase UK health spending by £24bn with mental health investment; scrap Bedroom Tax, raise Carer’s Allowance, overhaul sanctions/disability assessments; raise minimum wage to £8.70, promote Living Wage and equal pay audits; expand free childcare and keep university tuition free; climate targets, moratorium on fracking, expand renewables; protect HRA/ECHR, oppose mass surveillance, scrap House of Lords; oppose Trident renewal.\nTEXT_D: Extra £12bn for NHS, major boosts to nurses/GPs and £1.5bn for mental health/dementia; leave the EU with a British‑only referendum, prioritise British workers, national culture and English language, add national days; five years’ tax/NI before access to most benefits/NHS, Australian‑style points system, no amnesty for illegal migrants; increase police/prisons/border staff and defence spending ≥2% GDP, retain Trident, UK Bill of Rights and leave ECHR; raise personal allowance to ≥£13,000, enforce minimum wage and regulate zero‑hours, crack down on late payments; waive tuition fees for STEM for graduates who work and pay tax in the UK for five years.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly invoke Care via significant NHS and social support commitments, but TEXT_D also heavily emphasizes Loyalty (sovereignty, British culture, prioritising British workers) and Authority (more policing, defence, border control, domestic rights framework) while advancing Proportionality (contribution‑before‑benefits, points‑based immigration, STEM fee‑waiver tied to service). Given the electorate’s weights (Care highest, followed by Loyalty, Proportionality, and Authority), TEXT_D aligns more closely overall, whereas TEXT_C places relatively more emphasis on Equality, which has a lower weight in the profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
